<h2>Claim issues specific to Wimberley properties</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Metal roofs.</strong> Hail can dent standing-seam and exposed-fastener panels without causing leaks. Whether that is covered may hinge on a cosmetic damage exclusion. See <a href="/services/hail-damage-claims/">hail damage claims</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Trees on structures.</strong> Mature trees close to homes mean wind claims often involve tree impact, debris removal, and the limits that apply to each. See <a href="/services/wind-damage-claims/">wind damage claims</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Other structures.</strong> Barns, detached garages, workshops, and fences are usually covered under a separate, lower limit. Check your declarations page.</li>
<li><strong>Rental and guest properties.</strong> A cabin or home used as a rental may be insured under a landlord, dwelling, or commercial form rather than a standard homeowners policy, which changes what is covered.</li>
<li><strong>Mixed-cause losses.</strong> When wind, falling trees, and rising water all hit the same storm, each cause has to be documented on its own so the right policy responds.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Permits in Wimberley and unincorporated Hays County</h2>
<p>The City of Wimberley has its own permit office, separate from San Marcos. Check with the city before roof replacement, structural work, or rebuilding after a flood. Many homes with a Wimberley address are outside city limits, in unincorporated Hays County. The county does not issue residential building permits there, but it does regulate septic (OSSF), development, and floodplain matters, which can affect flood-damaged homes. Whether your insurer pays for code-related costs depends on your ordinance or law coverage; our <a href="/local-building-codes/">building codes guide</a> explains more.</p>

